<p>Russia and China vetoed a United Nations Security Council resolution on Tuesday that called on states to coordinate defensive efforts to protect navigation in the Strait of Hormuz. The move blocked the measure even though 11 members voted in favor, while Pakistan and Colombia abstained. The vote took place at UN headquarters in New York as the waterway remained disrupted during the Iran war, with global consequences because roughly one-fifth of the world’s oil normally passes through the strait. Bahrain drafted the resolution and revised it several times to win support, stripping out language that earlier versions reportedly tied to Chapter VII enforcement and instead stressing defensive coordination under international law. Russia and China still opposed the measure, arguing that it was biased against Iran or failed to reflect the region’s underlying dynamics, while the United States and France condemned the veto. The diplomatic setback came hours before a deadline set by President Trump for Iran to reopen the strait and reach a deal or face U.S. attacks on Iranian power plants and bridges. That timing underscored how quickly the crisis had escalated from maritime disruption to a great-power confrontation at the UN.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/gUP9CzG_pCvR9-02NOD3ld13NpLaDwoxZaGN0ncWNdNndwcjQ0w7QcQHjujSwKEsDvr8hGOCX7SAeyyYWNpWlNCLYRpkv11nMzIRUBls1btP' alt='Delegations from UN Security Council member states gather in the chamber ahead of a meeting at United Nations headquarters in New York on April 7.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Draft sponsor: Bahrain wrote the resolution in coordination with Gulf Cooperation Council states and Jordan, reflecting a regional push to make commercial shipping routes safer.</li><li>Final text: The last version urged countries that use the route to coordinate defensive measures, including escorting merchant vessels, and asked the UN secretary-general to brief the council within seven days and then every 30 days.</li><li>Voting lineup: Bahrain, the Democratic Republic of the Congo, Denmark, France, Greece, Latvia, Liberia, Panama, Somalia, the United Kingdom, and the United States voted yes, while Pakistan and Colombia abstained.</li><li>Oil exposure: The Strait of Hormuz is a strategic chokepoint for global energy shipments, so prolonged disruption there can quickly drive up energy costs worldwide.</li></ul><blockquote>Whole civilisation will die tonight - President Donald Trump</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Russia and China: Both governments opposed the resolution, saying it did not fairly address the broader regional context and, in Al Jazeera’s account, was biased against Iran. (<a href='https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/4/7/russia-and-china-block-un-resolution-on-strait-of-hormuz'>Al Jazeera</a>)</li><li>United States: Washington linked the UN vote to mounting pressure on Tehran, with President Trump setting an 8 p.m. Eastern deadline for Iran to reopen the waterway or face attacks on power plants and bridges. (<a href='https://www.pbs.org/newshour/world/russia-and-china-veto-watered-down-un-resolution-aimed-at-reopening-the-strait-of-hormuz'>PBS NewsHour</a>)</li><li>France: France joined the United States in condemning the veto after the watered-down text still failed to pass the council. (<a href='https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/4/7/russia-and-china-block-un-resolution-on-strait-of-hormuz'>Al Jazeera</a>)</li><li>Pakistan: Pakistan abstained rather than support or block the measure, putting it apart from both the majority backing the draft and the two veto-wielding opponents. (<a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1989533/china-russia-veto-un-resolution-to-reopen-strait-of-hormuz'>Dawn</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='http://www.euronews.com/2026/04/07/russia-and-china-veto-un-resolution-aimed-at-reopening-hormuz-strait-as-us-deadline-for-de'>Russia and China veto UN resolution aimed at reopening Hormuz strait as US deadline for deal looms</a> - euronews.com</li><li><a href='https://www.scmp.com/news/world/article/3349309/iran-war-russia-and-china-veto-un-resolution-reopen-strait-hormuz'>Iran war: Russia and China veto UN resolution aimed at reopening Strait of Hormuz</a> - scmp.com</li><li><a href='https://www.pbs.org/newshour/world/russia-and-china-veto-watered-down-un-resolution-aimed-at-reopening-the-strait-of-hormuz'>Russia and China veto watered-down UN resolution aimed at reopening the Strait of Hormuz</a> - pbs.org</li><li><a href='https://www.nytimes.com/2026/04/07/world/middleeast/un-resolution-veto-strait-of-hormuz-china-russia.html'>Russia and China Veto Proposed U.N. Resolution to Open the Strait of Hormuz</a> - nytimes.com</li><li><a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1989533/china-russia-veto-un-resolution-to-reopen-strait-of-hormuz'>China, Russia veto UN resolution to reopen Strait of Hormuz</a> - dawn.com</li><li><a href='https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/4/7/russia-and-china-block-un-resolution-on-strait-of-hormuz'>Russia and China veto a United Nations Security Council resolution calling on states to &quot;coordinate efforts, defensive in nature&quot; to ensure the safety of navigation in the Strait of Hormuz, saying that it was biased against Iran. The U.S. and France condemn the veto.</a> - aljazeera.com</li></ul>